                    Hsl("Torque LFE Mod", "0 – 2", "Scales the engine rumble by the actual engine torque. 0 = torque has no effect on the rumble; higher = rumble swells with the engine load."),
                    Hsl("Flatspot Vibration", "0 – 2", "A sharp pulse once per wheel revolution when a tyre has a flatspot. Higher = more pronounced thump-thump-thump."),
                    Hsl("Surface Feel", "0 – 2", "Rumble when the tyres leave the racing surface. Gravel reads coarse, grass reads softer. Higher = more surface detail."),
                    Hsl("Brake Pressure Feel", "0 – 2", "Brake-bite texture from per-wheel brake pressure — a subtle vibration under hard braking. Higher = more brake feel through the wheel."),
                    Hsl("TC Feel", "0 – 2", "A subtle force release when traction control cuts power. Higher = you feel each intervention as a small tug of the wheel."),
                    Hsl("Core Smoothing", "0 – 0.5", "Smoothing on the main force. 0 = raw; 0.15 = light, keeps steering feel; 0.5 = heavy, may feel sluggish."),
                    Hsl("Detail Smoothing", "0 – 0.8", "Smoothing on the texture effects. 0 = raw vibrations; 0.3 = moderate (calms jitter, keeps rumble strips); 0.8 = heavy, kills texture."),
                    Hsl("Grip Scale (LMU)", "0 – 1", "Le Mans Ultimate: how much centering force fades as the front tyres approach zero grip, using the game's real per-wheel grip fraction. 0 = always full force; 1 = full fade at zero grip."),
                    Hsl("Tyre Temp Feel (LMU)", "0 – 1", "Le Mans Ultimate: subtle texture from cold (under ~75°C) or overheated (over ~105°C) tyres. 0 = off; higher = more temperature texture.")),
                S("Vibrations",
                    P("All the vibration feedback mixed into the main force: kerbs, slip, road texture, ABS, off-track surfaces. Everything here scales with the Vibration Master."),
                    Hsl("Vibration Master", "0 – 10", "Master volume for all vibration effects — scales Curb, Slip, Road and ABS together. Turn this up first, then balance the individual sources."),
                    Hsl("Curb Gain", "0 – 10", "Kerb and rumble-strip strike intensity. Higher = the sharp rattle over sausage kerbs. Also scales kerb bumps in the main force."),
                    Hsl("Slip", "0 – 10", "Tyre-slip vibration intensity — the buzz when wheels spin or lock. Higher = more of the 'slipping' texture."),
                    Hsl("Road", "0 – 10", "Road-surface vibration intensity. Higher = the constant fine surface texture, like tarmac grain, is stronger."),
                    Hsl("ABS Intensity", "0 – 10", "ABS pulse vibration — and adds pulses to the main steering force when ABS kicks in. Higher = each ABS cycle is felt more clearly."),
                    Hsl("Suspension Road", "0 – 10", "Road texture derived from suspension movement, fed into the main force channel. Higher = more detailed surface feel from the suspension geometry."),
                    Hsl("Offtrack Gain", "0 – 2", "Overall intensity of off-track feedback. 0 = nothing off track, 0.5 = subtle, 1.0 = strong rumble over grass/gravel."),
                    Hsl("Offtrack Severity", "0 – 10", "How aggressively suspension movement is amplified off track. Higher = more violent rumble on grass and gravel."),
                    Hsl("ABS Pulse Strength", "0 – 1", "How punchy each ABS pulse feels. Gentle cars ≈ 0.10, aggressive cars ≈ 0.25–0.50.")),
                S("Damping & Friction",
                    P("Speed-sensitive steering resistance. Per-wheel games and Le Mans Ultimate only."),
                    Hsl("Speed Damping", "0 – 25", "Viscous damping that increases with car speed — the steering tightens as you go faster, like real power steering that firms up. Higher = heavier at speed. Scaled by Damping Speed Ref."),
                    Hsl("Friction", "0 – 20", "Constant resistance whenever you turn the wheel (Coulomb friction). Higher = heavier, more mechanical, 'coggy' feel; too high = numb and sticky."),
                    Hsl("Inertia", "0 – 20", "Simulated steering-column inertia: resists rapid direction changes, increasing with speed. Higher = the wheel feels heavier to flick side to side; helps stability but can dull flick entries."),
                    Hsl("Damping Speed Ref (km/h)", "50 – 500", "The speed at which speed-based damping reaches full strength. Lower = damping kicks in sooner (firm at lower speeds).")),
                S("Dynamic Effects",
                    P("Supplemental forces from G-forces and suspension movement."),
                    Hsl("Cornering Force", "0 – 5", "Force from lateral G-force in corners. Higher = more of the 'the car is leaning on the wheel' sensation in long, fast corners."),
                    Hsl("Road Feel", "0 – 5", "Force from rapid suspension compression changes — bumps and kerbs. Only reacts to changes, so it adds punch without adding constant heaviness."),
                    Hsl("Accel/Braking Force", "0 – 5", "Force from longitudinal G-force. Higher = you feel weight transfer under braking and acceleration as a pull on the wheel."),
                    Hsl("Car Rotation Force", "0 – 5", "Force from how fast the car is rotating (yaw rate). Higher = clearer feel of slides and direction transitions.")),
                S("LFE — Low-Frequency Effects",
                    P("A low-frequency rumble driven by suspension travel and speed — feels like chassis road texture, especially on strong wheelbases."),
                    B("Enable LFE — master switch."),
                    Hsl("LFE Gain", "0 – 1", "Overall intensity of the low-frequency effect in the main force."),
                    Hsl("Frequency", "3 – 30 Hz", "The base rumble frequency. Lower = deeper, heavier thump; higher = faster, buzzier."),
                    Hsl("Suspension Drive", "0 – 1", "How much suspension travel drives the effect envelope. Higher = more reactive to bumps."),
                    Hsl("Speed Scaling", "0 – 1", "How much vehicle speed modulates the frequency. Higher = the rumble speeds up with road speed."),
                    Hsl("RPM Drive", "0 – 1", "How much engine RPM drives intensity and frequency. Higher = more engine-vibration rumble at high revs.")),
                S("Signal Processing",
                    P("How the force behaves around the steering centre, plus smoothing and filtering."),
                    Hsl("Center Suppress (deg)", "0 – 30°", "A zone near centre where forces fade to zero with a quadratic ramp. Wider = a bigger 'quiet zone' around straight-ahead, killing any centre twitch. Only active when Sign Correction is on."),
                    Hsl("Noise Floor", "0 – 0.05", "Forces below this level are silenced. Higher = a still, quiet wheel when nothing is happening — but fine detail is lost too."),
                    Hsl("Center Knee Power", "0.5 – 3", "Shapes how force ramps up from centre. 1.0 = normal; higher = lighter right at centre with a sharper onset as you turn — a 'notchy then firm' feel."),
                    Hsl("Center Blend (deg)", "0 – 10°", "The angle over which lateral (cornering) force is suppressed near centre. Prevents Fy buzz driving straight. Higher = cornering weight comes in later."),
                    Hsl("Center Sharpness (deg)", "0 – 10°", "Column-force games: the angle at which centering force reaches full via a smooth curve. Lower = crisper, more defined on-centre feel; 0 = disabled (crispest)."),
                    B("Mute Gear Change FFB — suppresses force spikes during gear shifts. On by default; leave it on."),
                    Hsl("Steer Vel Reference", "1 – 50", "Reference speed for steering-velocity normalisation. Higher = less damping response to fast steering inputs (the wheel stays freer when you flick it)."),
                    Hsl("Velocity Deadzone", "0 – 0.5", "Minimum steering speed before damping engages. Higher = damping only during fast turns. Great anti-bounce-back control for the centre."),
                    Hsl("Low Speed Smooth (km/h)", "0 – 100", "Below this speed, all channel smoothing increases. Higher = silkier low-speed feel in slow corners, but less detail.")),
                S("Auto-Gain",
                    P("Automatically adjusts overall strength so different cars feel similar — compensates for cars with naturally heavy or light FFB."),
                    B("Enable Auto-Gain — master switch."),
                    Hsl("Auto-Gain Scale", "0.1 – 5", "The target level it compensates toward. Higher = stronger auto-compensation, more similar feel across cars.")),
                S("Wet Weather",
                    P("Adjusts the whole feel for wet tracks: suppresses vibration detail, widens slip curves, raises the noise floor, and can simulate hydroplaning. Enable before driving in rain. Per-wheel games only."),
                    B("Enable Wet Weather — master switch.",
                        "Auto-Detect Wet Conditions — the app reads track wetness from the game. When off, Manual Intensity is used instead."),
                    Hsl("Road Vibration", "0 – 1", "How much road-surface vibration is suppressed in the wet. 0 = full road feel, 0.7 = dampened, 1.0 = road feel removed — like the tyres are on a film of water."),
                    Hsl("Curb Vibration", "0 – 1", "How much kerb vibration is suppressed in the wet. Kerbs feel muted on wet tracks."),
                    Hsl("Tire Scrub", "0 – 1", "How much scrub texture is suppressed. Less grip = less scrub grain in the wet."),
                    Hsl("Manual Intensity", "0 – 1", "When auto-detect is off: the wetness level. 0 = dry, 1 = heavy rain."),
                    Hsl("Peak Slip Angle Mult", "1.0 – 3.0", "Widens the slip curve: 1.0 = dry curve, 1.6 = wider wet curve — peak grip happens at larger slip angles, so the limit feel comes later and softer."),
                    Hsl("Damping Reduction", "0 – 1", "How much speed damping is reduced in the wet. Wet tyres have less mechanical grip = less steering resistance."),
                    Hsl("Noise Floor Boost", "0 – 1", "How much the noise floor is raised in the wet. Masks the fine detail that would be lost on wet tarmac anyway."),
                    B("Enable Hydroplaning Feel — adds force loss at speed on standing water."),
                    Hsl("Speed Threshold (km/h)", "60 – 250", "Speed above which hydroplaning risk builds. Lower = earlier onset; higher = only at very high speed."),
                    Hsl("Max Attenuation", "0 – 0.8", "Maximum force reduction while hydroplaning. 0.3 = subtle lightness, 0.6 = noticeable force loss, 0.8 = severe aquaplaning — the wheel suddenly goes weightless."))
            ),

            A("Equalizer", "Shaping the final force signal by frequency — 10 bands and what they feel like",
                new[] { "equalizer", "eq", "band", "frequency", "hz", "boost", "cut", "texture", "weight", "curve" },
                S("What the equalizer does",
                    P("The equalizer shapes the final force signal right before it reaches the wheel — like an audio EQ, but for force. Each of the 10 vertical sliders covers a frequency range of the force signal. Boosting a band makes that kind of force stronger; cutting it removes it."),
                    P("Start flat (0 dB on every band) and adjust one band at a time while driving. The bands are tuned to the app's own effect frequencies, so each one maps to a recognisable feeling."),
                    Note("Tip", "A flat EQ with good master settings is often the best starting point. Use the EQ for broad 'character' changes (heavy vs light wheel, smooth vs detailed) rather than fixing a single effect — specific effects have their own sliders on the FFB Tuning page.")),
                S("The 10 bands",
                    Hsl("Steer Weight — 2 Hz", "-12 to +12 dB", "The very lowest, heaviest forces: overall steering resistance and parking-lot effort. Boost = a heavier, weightier wheel; cut = a lighter, freer wheel."),
                    Hsl("Weight Transfer — 4 Hz", "-12 to +12 dB", "Body roll, brake dive and acceleration squat. Boost = more sense of the car pitching and leaning under load changes."),
                    Hsl("Self Align — 8 Hz", "-12 to +12 dB", "The self-centering torque: how firmly the wheel returns to centre and how grip balance feels. Boost = stronger, more present centre; cut = a softer, more relaxed centre."),
                    Hsl("Steering Precision — 12 Hz", "-12 to +12 dB", "Small corrections and line tracking. Boost = the wheel feels more 'connected' and precise on minor inputs; too much can feel nervous."),
                    Hsl("Turn-In Response — 18 Hz", "-12 to +12 dB", "Initial bite and direction changes. Boost = a sharper, more eager response when you first turn the wheel into a corner."),
                    Hsl("Road Texture — 25 Hz", "-12 to +12 dB", "Tarmac grain and surface roughness. Boost = more of the constant fine surface detail; cut = a smoother, more 'polished' feel."),
                    Hsl("Bump Detail — 35 Hz", "-12 to +12 dB", "Patches, seams and road features. Boost = each surface imperfection is more distinct as you hit it."),
                    Hsl("Kerb Strike — 50 Hz", "-12 to +12 dB", "Kerb hits, potholes and sharp impacts. Boost = kerb strikes rattle harder and cut through the rest of the force."),
                    Hsl("Wheel Chatter — 70 Hz", "-12 to +12 dB", "Rapid vibration and wheel buzz — often the frequency of 'flutters' on coarse surfaces or under lock-up. Cut this band first if the wheel feels buzzy."),
                    Hsl("ABS/TC Pulses — 100 Hz", "-12 to +12 dB", "The finest vibration: ABS cycling and traction-control pulsing. Boost = assist interventions are clearly felt as rapid micro-pulses.")),
                S("Presets & practical use",
                    B("Use the Enable checkbox to A/B the whole EQ instantly.",
                        "A boost of +3 to +6 dB on Self Align and Steering Precision with a small cut on Wheel Chatter is a common 'crisp centre' starting point.",
                        "If the wheel feels harsh, cut the 50 Hz and 70 Hz bands first — they carry the harshest impacts.",
                        "If the wheel feels dead at low speed, boost the 2 Hz and 4 Hz bands."))
            ),

            A("FFB Coach", "The AI tuning assistant that reads your telemetry and recommends changes",
                new[] { "ffb coach", "ai", "assistant", "chat", "monitor", "snapshot", "analyze", "recommendation", "telemetry analysis" },
                S("What it is",
                    P("FFB Coach is an AI assistant that helps you tune. It reads real telemetry from your driving (or from a saved snapshot) and talks you through what the data shows — oscillation, snapping, clipping, weak centre — and suggests specific slider changes."),
                    P("The FFB Coach needs an AI provider configured first: open Settings → AI Coach and paste your OpenCode Go API key (from opencode.ai/auth). The default model (DeepSeek V4 Flash) is cheap to run.")),
                S("Ways to use it",
                    B("Chat with AI — a conversation where you describe a problem ('the wheel oscillates on straights') and it asks questions, then recommends changes.",
                        "Live Monitor — the coach watches your live telemetry while you drive and comments on what it sees.",
                        "Load Snapshot Data — analyse a saved snapshot (press your assigned snapshot button in-game, then load it here). This is the most reliable mode: the coach analyses real recorded signals."),
                    P("During a session the top bar shows the state (Thinking…, Chat Active, Applying, Summary). Restart clears the conversation; Summary produces a final write-up of recommended changes."),
                    Note("Trust but verify", "The coach's suggestions are applied to your profile and can always be reverted. Change one recommended slider at a time and drive before accepting the next."))
            ),

            A("Devices — LED Effects", "Wheel LED strips: brightness, flash effects, colors and RPM thresholds",
                new[] { "devices", "led", "lights", "rpm", "shift light", "flash", "brightness", "color", "color scheme", "threshold" },
                S("What this tab does",
                    P("The LED Effects tab controls the LED strip on your wheel — the shift lights. Which options are available depends on the wheel: some wheels only accept basic on/off LEDs, others (SDK-connected wheels) allow full RGB control and brightness."),
                    P("A live preview bar at the top shows what the LEDs will look like.")),
                S("Sections and controls",
                    Hsl("Brightness", "5 – 100%", "Overall LED brightness (SDK-connected wheels only). Lower = dimmer so it is less distracting at night."),
                    B("Shift / Limiter Flash — all LEDs flash at the redline or when the rev limiter cuts.",
                        "Pit Limiter Flash — alternating outer LEDs while the pit limiter is active.",
                        "ABS Flash — the first and last LED flash while ABS is working.",
                        "Race Flag Indicators — LEDs show yellow, red and blue race flags."),
                    Hsl("Flash Speed", "2 – 60", "How fast flashing effects blink. Lower = faster (2 is very fast, 60 is slow)."),
                    B("Color Scheme — for per-LED RGB wheels: Traffic Light (green/yellow/red), Blue Gradient, Red Hot, Monochrome, or Custom."),
                    B("RPM Thresholds — presets: Default (50–100%), Early (30–100%), Late (70–100%), Linear (10–100%), or Custom. Each of the 10 LEDs lights up when RPM crosses its own threshold (as a % of redline). Pick a preset, then switch to Custom to fine-tune individual LEDs."))
            ),

            A("Devices — Haptic Pad (HF8)", "The seat/back vibration pad: zone mapping, sources, and gains",
                new[] { "devices", "haptic", "hf8", "pad", "vibration", "seat", "back", "motor", "zone", "kerb", "suspension", "engine" },
                S("What this tab does",
                    P("The Haptic Pad tab controls a Next Level Racing HF8 (or HF8 Pro) seat pad: 8 vibration motors across your seat and lower back. You map telemetry sources — suspension, tyre slip, kerbs, lateral G, engine RPM — to each motor zone, so a kerb strike on the left side buzzes the left side of the seat."),
                    Note("Before it will connect", "Connect the HF8 via USB and close the NLR HFS software — it holds exclusive HID access and blocks this app.")),
                S("Master controls",
                    B("Haptic Output — master enable switch."),
                    Hsl("Master Gain", "0 – 1", "Overall intensity for all motor zones. Turn this up first, then balance zones."),
                    Hsl("Output Rate", "15 – 120 Hz", "The motor update rate: 75 Hz for the HF8 Pro, 15 Hz for the original HF8. Set it to match your hardware or the motors will feel wrong.")),
                S("The 8 zones",
                    P("Zones are laid out as four rows: Seat Front (left/right), Seat Rear (left/right), Back Lower (left/right), Back Upper (left/right). Each zone has:"),
                    Hsl("Zone Intensity", "0 – 1", "How strongly this individual zone vibrates."),
                    Hsl("Suspension", "0 – 3", "How much suspension travel feeds this zone — bumps and kerbs through the seat."),
                    Hsl("Tire Slip", "0 – 3", "How much tyre slip feeds this zone — wheelspin and slides."),
                    Hsl("Kerbs", "0 – 3", "How much kerb-strike vibration feeds this zone."),
                    Hsl("Lateral G", "0 – 3", "How much cornering G-force feeds this zone — you lean into the seat in long corners."),
                    Hsl("Engine RPM", "0 – 3", "How much engine revs feed this zone — a constant buzz that follows the RPM."),
                    B("Enabled — switches the zone on/off.",
                        "Copy from here / Copy → — copy one zone's whole setup to another zone so the left/right sides match."),
                    Note("Suggested start", "Front-left zone = Suspension 1.0 + Kerbs 1.0; front-right zone mirrored. Back zones = Lateral G 1.0. Engine RPM 0.5 across the back for a subtle rev buzz. Then raise Master Gain while driving until it feels right.")),
                S("Testing",
                    P("The Test Motors button opens a popup that fires individual motors so you can verify the pad is wired and seated correctly before driving."))
            ),

            A("Devices — Pedals", "Pedal haptics, physical pedal input, routing and calibration",
                new[] { "devices", "pedals", "pedal", "haptic", "brake", "gas", "clutch", "osoyoo", "arduino", "serial", "calibration", "deadzone", "abs", "tc" },
                S("What this tab does",
                    P("The Pedals tab controls vibration feedback in your pedals. It is built around an Osoyoo Arduino Uno (or compatible serial device) with brake and gas vibration motors, plus everything needed to use physical pedals as an input source.")),
                S("PEDAL HAPTIC DEVICE (connection)",
                    P("Pick the COM port your Arduino is on (Refresh lists the ports) and press Connect. The motor test panel lets you fire the brake and gas motors at an intensity (0–255) to confirm the wiring before driving — the telemetry feed is paused during tests.")),
                S("Physical Pedal Input",
                    P("When enabled, pedal values come from your physical pedal hardware instead of game telemetry. Keyboard simulation (W = gas, S = brake, A = clutch) is also available for testing. The LIVE PEDAL STATE panel shows which source is active and the live gas/brake/clutch positions.")),
                S("Haptic Output Routing",
                    P("Each effect routes through a pedal column, then that column's master gain scales the whole channel (0–2×)."),
                    Hsl("Brake — Master Gain", "0 – 2", "Overall volume of everything on the brake pedal."),
                    Hsl("Brake — ABS Pulse", "0 – 2", "How strongly the ABS pulsing is felt in the brake pedal — a rapid stutter under hard braking as ABS cycles."),
                    Hsl("Brake — Brake Pressure Hum", "0 – 2", "A constant hum proportional to brake pressure — you feel the brake 'load up' under your foot."),
                    Hsl("Brake — Road Texture", "0 – 2", "Road surface vibration through the brake pedal — tarmac grain and bumps under your foot."),
                    Hsl("Gas — Master Gain", "0 – 2", "Overall volume of everything on the gas pedal."),
                    Hsl("Gas — TC Ignition Cut", "0 – 2", "A pulse in the gas pedal when traction control cuts ignition — you feel the car fighting for grip."),
                    Hsl("Gas — Corner Scrub", "0 – 2", "Front tyre scrub sensation through the gas pedal when cornering hard."),
                    Hsl("Gas — Throttle Position", "0 – 2", "A constant buzz proportional to throttle position."),
                    Hsl("Gas — Engine RPM Buzz", "0 – 2", "Engine vibration buzz that follows the RPM, through the gas pedal."),
                    Hsl("Gas — Curb (Shared)", "0 – 2", "Kerb-strike vibration shared to the gas pedal — kerbs are felt under your foot too."),
                    Hsl("Clutch — Master Gain", "0 – 2", "Overall volume for the clutch column. Note: the current Osoyoo firmware drives brake + gas motors only, so the clutch channel is ready but needs a clutch-capable motor.")),
                S("Calibration",
                    P("Each pedal (gas, brake, clutch) has the same tuning set. Calibrate with the pedal at rest and fully pressed:"),
                    Hsl("Deadzone", "0 – 0.3", "How far the pedal must travel before it registers any input. Removes resting jitter from worn pots."),
                    Hsl("Min Range", "0 – 1", "The raw value that maps to 'pedal fully released'. Set from the resting position."),
                    Hsl("Max Range", "0 – 1", "The raw value that maps to 'pedal fully pressed'. Set from the full-press position."),
                    B("Invert — flips the direction if the pedal reads backwards.",
                        "Smoothing α — how much the signal is smoothed. Higher = smoother but laggier; too high and fast dabs feel mushy."),
                    P("Press Save Pedal Configuration to keep the calibration permanently."))
            ),

            A("Devices — Buttons", "Assigning wheel buttons for snapshots and panic stop",
                new[] { "devices", "buttons", "assign", "snapshot", "panic", "button box", "wheel button" },
                S("What this tab does",
                    P("Assigns physical buttons for two in-app actions.")),
                S("Snapshot button",
                    B("Press your chosen button while driving to auto-save a telemetry snapshot (a recording of the last seconds of force and telemetry data, saved to the snapshots folder for analysis).",
                        "Pick a button that is easy to reach mid-corner — you will be pressing it during interesting moments.")),
                S("Panic stop button",
                    B("Assign a button on a SEPARATE device (ideally a button box) as a hardware panic stop. Pressing it instantly zeroes all FFB output — an emergency brake for the wheel.",
                        "The panic device opens in non-exclusive mode, so it does not conflict with the game or your FFB wheel.",
                        "A hardware panic button is the safest possible setup — even if the app's window loses focus, the button still works."))
            ),

            A("Profiles", "Saving, organising and auto-loading your setups per car and track",
                new[] { "profiles", "profile", "save", "import", "export", "car match", "track match", "auto load", "browser", "json" },
                S("What this page does",
                    P("A profile is a complete snapshot of every slider setting, stored as a JSON file. This page manages them: creating, renaming, deleting, importing, exporting, and — most usefully — telling the app which profile belongs to which car or track.")),
                S("Current Profile & Detection",
                    B("Active Profile — the profile currently in use. Switch with the dropdown.",
                        "Car Match — the car model that auto-loads this profile when detected in-game. Use Detected fills it from the car you are currently driving.",
                        "Track Match — an optional track name for organising profiles by track.",
                        "Detected Car / Detected Track — what the app currently sees in your session.",
                        "Auto-load profile when car changes — the master switch for per-car auto-loading. Switching cars in-game then loads the matching profile automatically.")),
                S("Actions",
                    B("Save Current Profile — writes current settings into the active profile (becomes Save As when a built-in profile is selected, because built-ins cannot be overwritten).",
                        "Save As New Profile… — copies the current settings into a new named profile.",
                        "Rename Profile / Delete Profile — manage your own profiles. Built-in profiles cannot be renamed or deleted.")),
                S("Import / Export",
                    B("Export Profile — saves the active profile as a JSON file you can share or back up.",
                        "Import Profile — loads a profile JSON file and adds it to your collection.")),
                S("Profile Browser (right side)",
                    P("Browse all profiles with search, view modes (flat list, grouped by track, grouped by car), and game filter pills (All Games, AC EVO, RaceRoom, Assetto Corsa, Le Mans Ultimate). Click any profile to activate it instantly."),
                    Note("Built-in profiles", "Profiles marked BUILT-IN are the app's starting points for your car. Save As to create your own editable copy based on them. Profiles with an older format show a version badge — enable Auto Upgrade Profiles in Settings to convert them automatically."))
            ),

            A("Telemetry Profiler", "Live signal analysis: the chart, channels, and snapshot tools",
                new[] { "telemetry", "profiler", "chart", "graph", "snapshot", "export csv", "export analysis", "clipping", "force out", "raw ff" },
                S("What this page does",
                    P("A live, scrolling chart of the FFB pipeline signals — the same data the app uses to build the force. This is the window into what the wheel is being told to do, second by second. It is invaluable when diagnosing problems: oscillation shows up as a sawtooth wave, clipping as flat-topped spikes, weak centre as tiny signal near the middle.")),
                S("Toolbar & readouts",
                    B("Pop Out Overlay — opens the chart in a floating window you can position over the game.",
                        "Snapshot — saves the current telemetry window (CSV + analysis text + animated HTML replay) to the snapshots folder.",
                        "Export Analysis / Export CSV — save the visible data for deeper analysis or sharing.",
                        "Clear — resets the chart.",
                        "Live readouts: Speed, Force Out, Throttle, Brake, Clipping %, Min/Max force, Avg force, Steering angle, Raw FF, peak Mz/Fx/Fy, and the time window."),
                    P("The channel checkboxes (Output, Raw FF, Compress, Gain Out, Post Damp, Dynamic, Speed, Steer°, Throttle, Brake) toggle which signals are drawn, and the legend on the left identifies their colours.")),
                S("Reading the chart",
                    B("Output (orange) — the final force actually sent to the wheel.",
                        "Raw FF (green) — the game's raw force input.",
                        "Compress (cyan) — after compression/limiting stages.",
                        "Gain Out / Post Damp (magenta / orange-red) — later pipeline stages, useful when checking damping behaviour.",
                        "CLIP indicator — when the force hits the wheel's limit, shown as a red CLIP badge and flat-topped signal. Occasional clipping in heavy moments is normal; constant clipping means Output Gain is too high."),
                    P("The bottom strip shows the three most important physics channels live: MzFront, FxFront, FyFront, so you can watch the raw tyre forces while you drive."),
                    Note("Snapshot workflow", "When something feels wrong, press your snapshot button during the moment, then load the snapshot in FFB Coach or export the CSV to see exactly what the pipeline was doing. This is the recommended way to report bugs."))
            ),

            A("Track Map", "Recording track layouts, force heatmaps, and FFB diagnostics",
                new[] { "track map", "track", "map", "record", "lap", "heatmap", "diagnostics", "satellite", "waypoint", "corner", "recommendations" },
                S("What this page does",
                    P("Builds a map of the track as you drive, overlays your car position, records a force heatmap (where the wheel is working hardest), and runs an FFB diagnostics pass that flags suspicious force behaviour at each point on track.")),
                S("Recording a track",
                    B("Press Record Lap and drive a full lap (start/finish in the normal place). The app samples position and force at waypoints.",
                        "Stop, then Complete & Save to finalise. The map is remembered for that track (per game) and auto-detected next time you drive it.",
                        "Export / Import — share a recorded track profile (with heatmap) as a file.",
                        "Calibrate — aligns the recorded map to the track's GPS/satellite data. Live Mode — switches to the live satellite view."),
                    P("The status strip shows corner number, sector, corner count, laps, pit status, track progress, distance from centre, and on/off-track state.")),
                S("Map overlays",
                    B("Heatmap — colours the track line by force output; you can see exactly where the wheel is under the most load.",
                        "Edges — shows the recorded track edges.",
                        "Diagnostics — the FFB health pass: green dots = expected behaviour, yellow = notable event, red = suspicious (possible code/signal issue).",
                        "Satellite — real satellite imagery of the track (needs calibration and internet).")),
                S("FFB Diagnostics panel & Recommendations",
                    P("The left panel explains the dot colours and the verdict. When diagnostics are on, a Recommendations bar appears at the bottom with suggested profile changes — Apply Profile Changes applies them to your profile so you can A/B test immediately."),
                    Note("What 'suspicious' looks like", "Force snapping on a straight, oscillation with no steering input, or force pushing in the same direction as the wheel are classic red flags. Most dots green and at corners = normal driving."))
            ),

